# Build output
build/
build-dist/
out/

# Release staging produced by `build.ps1 -Package` and `package-mac.sh`. The ZIP
# is uploaded to a release page, not committed -- a binary in git is a binary in
# git forever.
dist/
*.zip
*.aex
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp
*.lib

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.dSYM/

# Adobe's AE SDK -- NEVER commit. Licensed by Adobe, redistribution not
# permitted. Download separately and point AE_SDK_ROOT at it.
sdk/

# The Slang toolchain. NOT committed: 147 MB of prebuilt binaries, and a binary in
# git is a binary in git forever -- the same rule dist/ is under. It is a pinned,
# reproducible download rather than something anyone edits, so the repo carries the
# VERSION and the fetch instructions instead of the bytes.
#
# Get it with:  cmake -P cmake/FetchSlang.cmake
# or point PLUGIN_SLANG_ROOT at an existing install. cmake/Slang.cmake finds either,
# and a machine with neither still configures, builds and tests -- it just compiles
# the checked-in generated CUDA instead of regenerating it.
tools/slang/

# Intel Open Image Denoise. NOT committed, for the same reason and under the same
# rule: 53 MB of prebuilt binaries, and a binary in git is a binary in git forever.
# The repo carries the VERSION and the pinned SHA256 instead of the bytes.
#
# Get it with:  cmake -P cmake/FetchOidn.cmake
# A machine without it configures, builds and tests exactly the same -- the
# integration is a RUNTIME load (src/engine/Denoiser.h), never a link dependency,
# so the only difference is that denoiserAvailable() answers false and the render
# is noisier. That is also what a user who prunes the install folder gets.
#
# NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS WHETHER THE RELEASE BUNDLES IT. PLAN.md says bundle,
# because the denoiser is on by default; packaging copies out of tools/oidn/.
tools/oidn/

# NotchLC writes this into whatever the working directory happens to be when its
# codec component loads -- so it reappears after being deleted, and it is always
# empty. Nothing to do with this project.
NotchLC-log.txt

